From: MahanHD <mahanheidari2003@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] give photos the preview's white balance

Photos came out with a strong colour cast (red under a warm lamp) while
the preview was right. Two things between them:

- on_output_changed() reset the white balance gains and black level on
  every mode change, and taking a photo switches to the capture mode, so
  the burst was processed and saved with gains of 1. They belong to the
  sensor and the scene: reset them only when the camera changes.
- postprocess.sh ran dcraw_emu without -w, so the JPEG ignored the DNG's
  white balance anyway; its plain dcraw branch already had it.

The gains reach the DNG through libdng_set_neutral(), which needs
recipe/libdng's fix to be written the right way round.

---
--- a/data/postprocess.sh	2026-10-12 23:48:20.413421847 +0400
+++ b/data/postprocess.sh	2026-10-12 23:48:20.416628393 +0400
@@ -50,11 +50,11 @@
 then
 	DCRAW=dcraw_emu
 	# -fbdd 1	Raw denoising with FBDD
-	set -- -fbdd 1
+	set -- -fbdd 1 -w
 elif [ -x "/usr/lib/libraw/dcraw_emu" ]; then
 	DCRAW=/usr/lib/libraw/dcraw_emu
 	# -fbdd 1	Raw denoising with FBDD
-	set -- -fbdd 1
+	set -- -fbdd 1 -w
 elif command -v "dcraw" > /dev/null
 then
 	DCRAW=dcraw
--- a/src/process_pipeline.c	2026-10-12 23:48:20.415066062 +0400
+++ b/src/process_pipeline.c	2026-10-12 23:48:20.463501914 +0400
@@ -1553,9 +1553,6 @@
                 gles2_debayer_use(gles2_debayer);
         }
 
-        state_proc.blacklevel = 0.0f;
-        state_proc.red = 1.0f;
-        state_proc.blue = 1.0f;
         gles2_debayer_configure(gles2_debayer,
                                 output_buffer_width,
                                 output_buffer_height,
@@ -1641,6 +1638,16 @@
         state_proc.flash_enabled = new_state->flash_enabled;
 
         if (camera_changed) {
+                /*
+                 * White balance and the black level belong to the sensor and
+                 * the scene, not to a mode: they used to be reset on every
+                 * mode change too, so a photo, which switches to the capture
+                 * mode, was taken with no white balance at all.
+                 */
+                state_proc.blacklevel = 0.0f;
+                state_proc.red = 1.0f;
+                state_proc.blue = 1.0f;
+
                 char cf[PATH_MAX];
                 if (find_calibration(sizeof(cf), cf, state_proc.camera->name)) {
                         state_proc.calibration = parse_calibration_file(cf);
